WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.542
Authentication answers who or what is
calling.

2
00:00:02.622 --> 00:00:05.071
Authorization answers what that caller is
allowed to do.

3
00:00:05.151 --> 00:00:08.571
A secure Go service architecture keeps
those questions separate and explicit.

4
00:00:08.651 --> 00:00:11.917
A caller can be known and still not be
allowed to perform a particular action.

5
00:00:11.997 --> 00:00:14.447
Go services may need to handle several
kinds of identity.

6
00:00:14.527 --> 00:00:16.620
A user may act through a browser or mobile
client.

7
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:19.660
Another service may call through
machine-to-machine credentials.

8
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.526
A background job may process queued work.

9
00:00:21.606 --> 00:00:24.158
An administrative tool may perform a
sensitive operation.

10
00:00:24.238 --> 00:00:27.504
Each caller type needs a clear identity
model and a clear path for permission

11
00:00:27.584 --> 00:00:28.095
decisions.

12
00:00:28.175 --> 00:00:30.982
Authorization should be enforced close to
the protected action.

13
00:00:31.062 --> 00:00:34.583
A gateway, router, or middleware layer may
authenticate a request and reject many

14
00:00:34.663 --> 00:00:38.134
invalid calls early, but the code that
performs a sensitive read, write, export,

15
00:00:38.214 --> 00:00:41.736
or administrative change should still make
the permission requirement visible.

16
00:00:41.816 --> 00:00:45.184
This protects the service when routes are
reorganized, new callers are added,

17
00:00:45.264 --> 00:00:46.438
or internal code is reused.

18
00:00:46.518 --> 00:00:47.794
Token claims, roles, scopes,

19
00:00:47.874 --> 00:00:50.630
and service credentials should be treated
as sensitive inputs.

20
00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.231
The service should validate the issuer,
audience, expiration, expected format,

21
00:00:54.311 --> 00:00:56.455
and relevant claims according to its trust
model.

22
00:00:56.535 --> 00:01:00.006
It should also avoid logging credentials
or copying sensitive claim values into

23
00:01:00.086 --> 00:01:01.515
places where they are not needed.

24
00:01:01.595 --> 00:01:04.147
Machine-to-machine trust should be
explicit and limited.

25
00:01:04.227 --> 00:01:06.625
Credentials should have clear purpose,
narrow access,

26
00:01:06.705 --> 00:01:09.461
rotation expectations where appropriate,
and observable use.

27
00:01:09.541 --> 00:01:10.868
If service A can call service B,

28
00:01:10.948 --> 00:01:14.215
engineers should understand which
operations are allowed and how misuse or

29
00:01:14.295 --> 00:01:15.928
misconfiguration would be detected.

30
00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:19.785
Good architecture avoids spreading
authorization logic randomly across handlers and

31
00:01:19.865 --> 00:01:20.681
helper functions.

32
00:01:20.761 --> 00:01:23.109
Central policy helpers, clear
service-layer checks,

33
00:01:23.189 --> 00:01:26.047
and consistent audit events make
authorization easier to review.

34
00:01:26.127 --> 00:01:29.240
The team should be able to answer, for any
protected action, who can do it,

35
00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:32.740
which code enforces it, and what evidence
is recorded when the decision is made.
